%I A402033 #40 Nov 25 2026 20:12:41
%S A402033 1,2,3,4,5,6,10,17,34,257,514,65537,131074
%N A402033 Numbers k such that every invertible residue modulo k, except 1, has a positive power congruent to k-1 modulo k.
%C A402033 Includes all the Fermat primes A019434.
%C A402033 From _Jianing Song_, Nov 25 2026: (Start)
%C A402033 Numbers k such that the multiplicative group of integers (Z/kZ)* modulo k is cyclic of order a power of 2.
%C A402033 Proof: Let k >= 3. Write (Z/kZ)* as an internal product C_{2^a_1} X ... X C_{2^a_r} X G, with a_1, ..., a_r >= 1, and G being a group of odd order. Let g_i be a generator of C_{2^a_i}, then we have -1 = Product_{i=1..r} g_i^e_i, where e_i = 0 or 2^(a_i-1), and at least one of e_i is not equal to 0. Suppose that e_1 = 2^(a_1-1), then no element in the subgroup C_{2^a_2} X ... X C_{2^a_r} X G has a power equal to -1. As a result, the condition of k being a term of this sequence implies that C_{2^a_2} X ... X C_{2^a_r} X G is trivial, i.e., (Z/kZ)* = C_{2^a_1} is cyclic of order a power of 2.
%C A402033 Union of {1, 2, 4}, A019434, and 2*A019434. (End)
%e A402033 For k = 10, the invertible residues are 1, 3, 7, 9. We have:
%e A402033   3^2 == 9 = 10-1 (mod 10),
%e A402033   7^2 == 9 = 10-1 (mod 10),
%e A402033   9^1 == 9 = 10-1 (mod 10).
%e A402033 Thus 10 belongs to the sequence.
%e A402033 For k = 12, the invertible residues are 1, 5, 7, 11. However, neither 5 nor 7 have a power congruent to k-1 modulo 12. Thus 12 does not belong to the sequence.
%o A402033 (PARI) row(n) = select(x->gcd(n, x)==1, [2..n]);
%o A402033 isok2(i, k) = for (j=1, k, if (Mod(i,k)^j == k-1, return(1)););
%o A402033 isok(k) = my(v=row(k)); for (i=1, #v, if (!isok2(v[i], k), return(0));); 1; \\ _Michel Marcus_, Nov 12 2026
%Y A402033 Cf. A033948, A019434, A002322.
%K A402033 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A402033 1,2
%A A402033 _Leo Hennig_, Nov 11 2026
%E A402033 a(1) = 2 inserted and a(11) added by _Michel Marcus_, Nov 12 2026
%E A402033 a(1) = 1 prepended and a(13) from _Sean A. Irvine_, Nov 18 2026
